# Fable Advisor — postures, roles, fill table (Cursor)

Any model may be the main agent; posture, not model identity, decides how it works. Doctrine lives in the fable-advisor:orchestration skill (`lanes-cursor.md` for this harness); this file carries the user-side inputs only. Dated 2026-09-11; anchored to Grok 4.6 / GPT-6 Astra / GPT-5.6 Luna / Opus 5 / Fable 5.1 — re-evaluate an entry when any model it involves changes generation.

## Posture

Two postures, differing in exactly one rule: whether the main agent edits deliverables itself. Dispatching any role (explorer / worker / advisor) is available in both.

- **Orchestrating**: write delivery contracts, dispatch workers, accept on evidence; never edit deliverables (the repo's `AGENTS.md` maps the classes). Coordination artifacts are written directly.
- **Implementing**: edit deliverables directly; still dispatch explorers, workers, advisors as useful.
- Selector, in order: my declaration this session ("直接改" → implementing; "派活 / 编排" → orchestrating) → an upstream instruction in the prompt → default: an upstream task artifact exists (`.scratch/<feature>/issues/`, spec, Trellis task) → orchestrating; none → implementing.
- Posture is relative to a dispatch: a lane is implementing for its own contract and orchestrating toward subagents it spawns. Depth is not limited.

## Decision-type gates (any main agent, any tier)

Consult the advisor (`fable-advisor`, decision shape) before: an architecture, data migration, API shape, or refactor strategy; overturning an established plan; changing a public interface or cross-module dependency; relaxing acceptance criteria; the same problem failing twice. Before declaring a multi-step deliverable done, use the advisor's acceptance shape (contract + diff + receipt). No other mandatory review points; no per-diff review.

## Cursor dispatch mechanics

- `worker` = `generalPurpose` + an explicit `model` from this turn's allowlist. Omitting `model` is a same-model dispatch (doctrine prose in the orchestrating posture), not a vendor lane.
- `explorer` = `explore` (+ explicit `model` when a specific family is wanted), or a runner in report mode.
- `fable-advisor` = the named agent; the user-level gate denies a missing or `inherit` `model`; any explicit family passes.
- GPT family = the codex runner through the Shell tool (`plugin/scripts/run-codex.mjs`, pending/receipt flow). No receipt gate here — fail open; judge the receipt yourself. Run it in an isolated worktree when other lanes write to the same tree concurrently.
- Plugin frontmatter `model:` is ignored; effort is pinned to the slug. Use the live allowlist slug, never a skill example that may be absent.

## Fill table — (role, tier) → candidates, best first

| Cell | Fills |
|---|---|
| explorer @ light | grok-4.6[medium] via grok runner report mode › gpt-5.6-luna[high] via codex runner report mode › `explore` inherit |
| explorer @ standard / senior | same as worker fills of that tier, in report mode |
| worker @ light | Grok-family slug (grok lane) › gpt-5.6-luna[high] via codex runner (only on my declaration or when the task is simple and GPT is wanted) |
| worker @ standard | Grok-family slug › Opus-family slug via `generalPurpose` |
| worker @ senior | gpt-6-astra[medium] via codex runner; escalate to [high] for unusually hard work. Takeover contract = original contract + prior report + receipt, fresh session |
| advisor (default senior) | Fable-family slug via `fable-advisor` › gpt-6-astra[high] via codex runner report mode. Any tier is allowed |

Notes: effort defaults per role: worker medium, explorer medium, advisor high.

